course: dictionary
lesson: 9318
title: Spelling
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Spelling is the way words are written, with the letters in the right order. Good spelling helps other people read your writing easily, and it means the same word always looks the same.
explain: Spelling is also a skill you practise. A useful method is look, say, cover, write, check: look at the word, say it, cover it, write it from memory, then check your version against the original.
explain: When you check your writing, hunt for small slips like spelling mistakes. Proofreading means finding these slips, while editing means improving the whole piece, such as choosing a stronger word. Both help your writing get better.
fact: Spelling is the way words are written with letters in the right order.
fact: Spelling is also the skill of writing words correctly.
fact: Some spelling rules are "i before e, except after c", and doubling a consonant before adding -ing, as in "hop" and "hopping".
fact: «American spelling dropped the u from words like "color" and "favorite"», helped by Noah Webster's dictionary of 1828; Britain kept "colour" and "favourite", and both spellings are correct in their own country.
fact: In Britain, "spellings" can mean a list of words to learn to spell for a test.
exercise: What is spelling? | *The way words are written with letters in the right order | A loud way of reading aloud in class | A kind of drawing | The number of pages in a book
exercise: Which of these is an American spelling? | colour | organise | *color | centre
exercise: In the method look, say, cover, write, check, what is the last step? | *Check your spelling against the original | Say the word out loud | Look at the word again | Cover the word with your hand
test: In Britain, what are "spellings"? | A kind of pencil | *A list of words to learn to spell for a test | A bag of letters for a game | A page from a storybook
test: In the word "becuase", what is wrong? | It has no capital letter | *The letters are out of order, so it is a spelling mistake | It is too long to be a word | It is a noun, not a verb
exam: Which is the best way to make sure a word is spelled right? | *Check it in a dictionary or against a list | Ask a friend who is also not sure | Write it in capital letters | Guess and hope it is correct
